Subject: Report base classes through `ParseCallbacks`

A base class reaches bindgen's output only as the field generated to store it,
and `Base::requires_storage` declines to generate that field twice over: for a
virtual base, because the object reaches it indirectly rather than at a fixed
offset, and for a base bindgen finds zero-sized, because a field would give it
a size. Both are right for the struct bindgen emits, and both leave the
inheritance unrecorded. `struct Derived : Empty { int x; };` comes out
`struct Derived { x: c_int }`, indistinguishable from a class with no bases at
all.

Only bindgen has that fact. `mod ir` is private, `DiscoveredItem::Struct`
carries names alone, no `ParseCallbacks` method mentions bases, and even
`CompInfo`'s `Trace` edges do not say which kind of base an edge leads to. A
consumer which emits C++ of its own - deciding whether a class is abstract,
which special members the language implicitly declares or deletes for it,
whether an upcast exists, whether the layout is the layout of the fields - has
nothing to work from but those fields, so it gets each of those wrong in
silence rather than loudly.

`denote_base_class` reports every base of every struct codegen writes members
for: the class inherited from, whether the inheritance is virtual, the access
specifier, and the name of the field storing the base where there is one. It
fires before the `requires_storage` skip, so the two invisible cases are
exactly what it adds. No generated output changes, and a consumer which does
not implement it is unaffected.

Reporting the access specifier rather than a boolean means `Base` carries a
`Visibility` in place of its `is_pub` flag, so that protected and private
inheritance stay distinguishable to a consumer; `Base::is_public` is unchanged
for bindgen's own use.

